---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Fleet tracking demo | Zakaria Zerrouk"
  description="Case study: a simulated fleet and the logic that turns raw GPS positions into geofence alerts, trips, stops and reports."
>
  <article class="case mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-24 pt-12">
    <p><a href="/#projects">← All projects</a></p>

    <h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Fleet tracking demo</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted">
      A small simulated fleet, and the code that turns raw GPS positions into
      geofence alerts, trips, stops and a per-vehicle report. It runs in the
      browser, and as a real pipeline with MQTT and PostgreSQL.
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-sm">
      <a href={site.demo} rel="noopener">Live demo</a> ·
      <a href={site.demoRepo} rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>

    <img
      src="/images/fleet-demo.png"
      alt="The demo: a map of Casablanca and Mohammedia with five truck and van icons, a list of geofence alerts on the left and a fleet report table underneath."
      width="1581"
      height="1207"
      loading="lazy"
      class="mt-6 h-auto w-full rounded-lg border border-border"
    />

    <h2>Why I built it</h2>
    <p>
      In my previous job I worked on fleet-tracking software, and that code
      isn't mine to show. This project covers the same kind of problem with
      original code and invented data: turning a stream of GPS points into
      events someone can act on.
    </p>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Nobody wants raw GPS points. A fleet operator wants to know when a
      vehicle left the depot, where it stopped and for how long, and how far it
      drove. Getting from positions to those answers means deciding what counts
      as a stop, when a vehicle has really left a zone, and what to do with
      messages that arrive late or twice.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Moves five vehicles along looping routes around Casablanca and Mohammedia, with simulated GPS noise.</li>
      <li>Raises an alert when a vehicle enters or leaves a circular zone (the depot).</li>
      <li>Detects stops and the trips between them.</li>
      <li>Totals trips, distance, driving time and stopped time per vehicle.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>My part</h2>
    <p>
      This is an independent project: original code, made-up data, nothing from
      an employer. I built it with AI assistance, and I ran, checked and can
      explain every part of it.
    </p>

    <h2>How it's built</h2>
    <pre>Browser demo:     simulator → detectors → map, alerts, report

Docker pipeline:  publisher → Mosquitto → ingest → PostgreSQL
                  (simulator)  (MQTT broker)  (validate + detectors)</pre>
    <p>
      All the detection logic lives in one TypeScript package of plain
      functions that know nothing about the browser, MQTT or the database. The
      browser demo and the ingest service call the same code, and I test it on
      its own. The map is MapLibre with OpenStreetMap tiles.
    </p>

    <h2>Decisions and trade-offs</h2>

    <h3>Stops come from dwell time, not speed</h3>
    <p>
      Speed worked out from two GPS points is unreliable: a parked vehicle that
      drifts 5 m in a second looks like it's doing 18 km/h. So a stop is a
      vehicle that stays within 50 m of one spot for 3 minutes. The cost is
      delay, since a stop can only be confirmed 3 minutes after it starts.
    </p>

    <h3>Trip times are reported honestly</h3>
    <p>
      Because of that delay, a trip is only known to have ended 3 minutes after
      the vehicle arrived. I give it the time the vehicle actually arrived, not
      the time the system noticed. This was the part that needed the most
      thought, because it makes the results arrive late but correct, and the
      report can lag the map by a few minutes.
    </p>

    <h3>Leaving a zone needs a buffer</h3>
    <p>
      GPS jitter around a zone boundary would otherwise produce a stream of
      fake enter and exit events. A vehicle has to get 20 m beyond the edge
      before it counts as having left. A test fails if that buffer is set to
      zero, which is how I know it's doing the work.
    </p>

    <h3>Late, duplicate and restarted streams</h3>
    <p>
      A position at or before the vehicle's last timestamp is dropped. One that
      is more than an hour earlier is treated as the stream restarting: the
      open trip or stop is saved and the vehicle starts fresh. The ingest
      service also rejects bad JSON, out-of-range values and messages where the
      topic and the vehicle id disagree.
    </p>

    <h3>What I left out on purpose</h3>
    <p>
      I don't store raw positions. At the default speed that would be over
      400,000 rows a day and nothing here needs them, but it means history
      can't be replayed. There's no Kafka, because a fleet this size doesn't
      need it, and no ORM, because four small tables are fine with plain SQL.
    </p>

    <h3>Two ways to run it</h3>
    <p>
      The hosted demo runs the simulation and detectors in the browser, so it's
      free to host and has nothing to break. The Docker version uses a real
      broker and database. The catch is that the hosted demo only simulates the
      pipeline. It doesn't use MQTT or PostgreSQL.
    </p>

    <h2>Evidence it works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Around 60 unit tests cover the logic. Some expected values are worked
        out by hand, for example a simulated 3 km route and the exact trips,
        stops and geofence events it must produce.
      </li>
      <li>
        I ran the whole pipeline in Docker and checked what landed in
        PostgreSQL. The first trips matched my own calculation of the
        straight-line distance between the route points, for example 9,001 m
        stored for a leg of about 9.0 km.
      </li>
      <li>
        I restarted the broker while everything was running. The ingest service
        reconnected, resubscribed and carried on saving.
      </li>
      <li>The <a href={site.demo} rel="noopener">live demo</a> is deployed and runs in your browser.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It's a demo with synthetic data, not production software.</li>
      <li>Vehicles drive straight lines at constant speed, so on the map they sometimes cut across buildings.</li>
      <li>A trip can end up to about 50 m short of where the vehicle actually stopped.</li>
      <li>Detector state is in memory, so restarting the ingest service loses any trip or stop in progress. A failed database write loses that message's results.</li>
      <li>Delivery is at most once, there's one ingest instance, and there's no authentication.</li>
      <li>The publisher and ingest services have no automated tests. I checked them by running the pipeline.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href={site.demoRepo} rel="noopener">README</a> lists these in full.
    </p>

    <h2>What I'd do next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Route vehicles along real roads instead of straight lines.</li>
      <li>Support polygon zones, not just circles.</li>
      <li>Keep detector state in a store so a restart doesn't lose anything in progress.</li>
      <li>Add automated tests for the publisher and ingest services, and authentication on the broker.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>